Q: Why is using pthread_mutex_lock and pthread_cond_wait inside a signal handler causing missed signals? A: <p>This answer addresses just one particular issue in the signal handler.</p>
<p>This code</p>
<pre><code>static int flag;

    while (flag) {
        /* Artificial delay to widen race window */
        for (volatile unsigned i = 0; i &lt; 100000; ++i) {
            __asm__ __volatile__(&quot;&quot; ::: &quot;memory&quot;);
        }

        /* ❌ NOT async-signal-safe */
        pthread_cond_wait(&amp;cond, &amp;mutex);
    }
</code></pre>
<p>is identical under <a href="https://port70.net/%7Ensz/c/c11/n1570.html#5.1.2.3p6" rel="nofollow noreferrer">the &quot;as-if&quot; rule</a> to</p>
<pre><code>static int flag;

    if (flag) {
        for ( ;; ) {
            /* Artificial delay to widen race window */
            for (volatile unsigned i = 0; i &lt; 100000; ++i) {
                __asm__ __volatile__(&quot;&quot; ::: &quot;memory&quot;);
            }

            /* ❌ NOT async-signal-safe */
            pthread_cond_wait(&amp;cond, &amp;mutex);
        }
    }
</code></pre>
<p>NB that the signal handler does not have to check <code>flag</code> more than once because it's never modified by the loop.</p>
<p>Given that the posted code <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79880791/why-is-using-pthread-mutex-lock-and-pthread-cond-wait-inside-a-signal-handler-ca#comment140966888_79880791">apparently does not reproduce the actual problem</a>, and it is almost certain that the problem code is compiled differently than the posted code, this lack of consistency could all too easily contribute to significant issues with this code.</p>
<p>Given all the other issues noted <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79880986/4756299">in John Bollinger's answer</a>, I'd say the entire implementation needs to be entirely rewritten without any attempts to handwave away undefined behavior.</p>
<p>Trying to reason around problems with undefined behavior in order to avoid having to actually fix problems is a fool's errand.  The undefined behavior can just manifest itself differently the next time the code is compiled because <em>something</em> changed somewhere.  Or it can reappear after the next system update when libraries and kernels change.  Or the phase of moon, the particular state of tides in the Bay of Fundy, or even the migrations of European swallows can trigger new failures caused by the undefined behavior.</p>
<p>Nevermind the fact that all the testing in the world that &quot;proves the solution works&quot; despite the presence of undefined behavior actually does nothing of the kind. That belief demonstrates a fundamental misunderstanding because not observing failure is not proof of proper functionality under any and all conditions.</p>
